const e='<h2>What you\'re building</h2>\n\n  <p>The target setup: phone → USB-C hub → monitor over HDMI, external SSD over USB, charger over power delivery. One connector on the phone, three things attached, and the phone charging the whole time.</p>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/files-external-drive.webp" alt="A file manager browsing a mounted external SSD, with iCloud Drive and local folders in the sidebar." width="1600" height="966" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>An SSD mounted alongside iCloud Drive and folders from the phone.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <h2>Choosing a hub</h2>\n\n  <p>Three things matter and nothing else does:</p>\n\n  <div class="table-scroll">\n    <table>\n      <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>What to look for</th></tr></thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr><td>HDMI out</td><td>4K30 is plenty; 4K60 if your monitor is high-refresh and you care</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Power delivery pass-through</td><td>Non-negotiable. Driving a display and a drive drains a phone quickly</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>USB data port</td><td>USB-A and USB-C both work; USB 3 speeds if you\'ll move large files</td></tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>You can ignore Ethernet, since your phone has a data plan. Ignore SD readers unless you shoot on cards. Ignore anything advertising "dual display", because a phone drives one external display and that\'s that.</p>\n\n  <p>Do be careful at the bottom of the market. Very cheap unbranded hubs are the usual cause of a monitor that flickers or a drive that keeps disconnecting. This is the one component where the middle of the market beats the bottom by a wide margin.</p>\n\n  <h2>Choosing a drive</h2>\n\n  <p>Bus-powered SSDs are the right answer: small, fast, no separate power brick, and a phone can supply what they need. Large spinning drives often can\'t be powered from a phone at all and will want their own supply. For format, exFAT is the safe universal choice, FAT32 works for small drives, and APFS and HFS+ generally work too. As a rule of thumb, if the iOS Files app can see the drive, XeOS can mount it.</p>\n\n  <h2>Making the drive usable</h2>\n\n  <p>iOS can <em>see</em> a drive in its Files app, but that\'s a picker rather than a workspace. <strong>XeOS: External Display Browser</strong> mounts folders properly. They get a permanent place in a sidebar, they survive restarts, and files inside them open in real viewers instead of quick previews.</p>\n\n  <ol class="steps">\n    <li>Plug the drive into the hub.</li>\n    <li>Open <strong>Files</strong> in XeOS and click <strong>Add Folder</strong> in the sidebar.</li>\n    <li>Navigate to the drive in the picker and choose the folder you want.</li>\n    <li>It appears in the sidebar with a drive icon, and stays there.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <figure class="is-narrow is-tall">\n    <img src="/content/images/files-add-folder.webp" alt="The Add Folder row at the bottom of the Files sidebar." width="432" height="1304" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>One row mounts local folders, iCloud Drive folders and external drives alike.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <p>Unplug the drive later and the location isn\'t lost. It\'s marked unavailable, with a <strong>Check Again</strong> action for when you reconnect.</p>\n\n  <h2>Mounting iCloud Drive too</h2>\n\n  <p>The same mechanism mounts an iCloud Drive folder, and this is where it stops being about a drive and starts being about a workflow. Your working folder from iCloud on one side, the SSD on the other, drag and drop between them.</p>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/files-icloud.webp" alt="A mounted iCloud Drive folder browsed in the file manager." width="1600" height="966" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>iCloud Drive mounted as a working directory rather than a picker destination.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <p>Files that haven\'t downloaded from iCloud yet still show up in the listing and get fetched when you open them.</p>\n\n  <div class="note">\n    <p><strong>One iOS rule to know.</strong> You can\'t mount the whole "On My iPhone" store, because iOS treats it as a provider root that apps aren\'t allowed to enumerate. Mount the level below, On My iPhone › Documents for example, and everything works. iCloud Drive isn\'t affected: it mounts fine at the root.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2>What you can do once it\'s mounted</h2>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>Open PDFs, images, video, text and Office files in place, in real viewers.</li>\n    <li>Drag and drop between locations, with progress for large copies.</li>\n    <li>Zip and unzip with no separate tool. Double-clicking a <code>.zip</code> extracts it in place.</li>\n    <li>Rename, duplicate, compress and delete from the right-click menu.</li>\n    <li>Slideshow a folder of images full screen.</li>\n    <li>Search across every mounted location from the Option Space overlay.</li>\n    <li>Pin a folder to the dock so it\'s one click away.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/files-images-folder.webp" alt="A folder of images shown as thumbnails in the file manager." width="1600" height="966" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>Thumbnails, list view and sorting on a mounted folder.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <h2>A realistic power-user day</h2>\n\n  <ol class="steps">\n    <li>Phone into the hub. Monitor, SSD and charger into the hub.</li>\n    <li>XeOS opens on the monitor with last session\'s windows already in place.</li>\n    <li>Card or SSD contents mounted in one sidebar, iCloud working folder in another.</li>\n    <li>Copy across, review full screen, annotate a PDF, write the accompanying notes in a browser window beside it.</li>\n    <li>Unplug. Everything\'s on the SSD and in iCloud.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h2>Limits</h2>\n\n  <p>Your other iPhone apps don\'t run inside the desktop. You get the browser, the built-in apps, and websites you install. A phone also supplies limited bus power, and two hungry devices on a cheap hub is exactly where trouble starts. Sustained large transfers are limited by the hub and the drive at least as much as by anything on the phone.</p>\n\n  <div class="cta">\n    <p><strong>XeOS: External Display Browser</strong> turns an iPhone or iPad into a full desktop on any monitor, with real file management for iCloud Drive, local folders and external drives.</p>\n    <p><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/external-display-browser/id6758286241" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="app-store-link">Download XeOS on the App Store</a></p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class="faq">\n    <h2>Frequently asked</h2>\n\n    <h3>Do I need to re-grant drive access every time?</h3>\n    <p>No. A mounted location is remembered across restarts.</p>\n\n    <h3>What drive format should I use?</h3>\n    <p>exFAT is the safest universal choice. If the iOS Files app can read the drive, this can mount it.</p>\n\n    <h3>Will a hard drive work, or only SSDs?</h3>\n    <p>Bus-powered SSDs are the reliable option. Larger spinning drives usually need their own power supply.</p>\n\n    <h3>Can I edit a file on the drive directly?</h3>\n    <p>Yes. You\'re working on the real file, and saves write back in place.</p>\n  </div>\n';export{e as default};
